# Security audit allowlist for dev-only dependencies.
# Enforced by .ci/scripts/security/audit.sh (two-pass strategy):
#
#   Pass 1 — production deps (--omit=dev): ZERO TOLERANCE, no allowlist.
#            Any severity → hard fail.
#   Pass 2 — dev deps: this allowlist applies.
#
# Rules:
#   - NEVER add production dependency advisories here; they cannot be allowlisted.
#   - Only add dev-dependency advisories after confirming no upgrade path exists.
#   - Each group MUST have a "# BLOCKER: <reason>" line; the audit script
#     enforces this strictly. Blank lines separate groups.
#
# Currently EMPTY, and that is the correct state. Every previous entry
# (tar, minimatch, yauzl, brace-expansion, tmp, node-tar — 18 advisory IDs
# across 6 groups) was justified by an electron / electron-builder chain.
# Electron is gone from the tree: package-lock.json contains no electron,
# electron-builder, electron-vite, electron-updater, electron-publish,
# app-builder-lib or @electron/* entry, and none of those 18 advisories
# appears in `npm audit` output any more. Do not re-add an entry without
# confirming the advisory actually fires.

# BLOCKER: brace-expansion GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg (unbounded expansion LENGTH -> OOM).
# DEV-ONLY: the remaining vulnerable copies are the six eslint/minimatch@3 edges
# (eslint-plugin-import/jsx-a11y/react, @eslint/config-array, @eslint/eslintrc)
# pinned to 1.1.16 by the brace-expansion@^1.1.7 override. The top-level v5 edge
# IS fixed (override moved 5.0.7 -> 5.0.8 in this change), so this covers only
# the 1.x line.
# NO UPGRADE PATH EXISTS, verified rather than assumed:
#   1. The advisory patches at 5.0.8 ONLY; the 1.x line ends at 1.1.16
#      (2026-07-08) with no 1.1.17, and every 1.x/2.x/3.x/4.x release is inside
#      the declared "<= 5.0.7" range.
#   2. Forcing those edges to 5.0.8 crashes eslint. Tested directly against
#      5.0.8 in a scratch package: `require('brace-expansion')` returns
#      { EXPANSION_MAX, EXPANSION_MAX_LENGTH, expand } and calling it throws
#      "e is not a function" -- which is exactly what minimatch@3.1.5 does at
#      minimatch.js:10 (`var expand = require('brace-expansion')`) and :271
#      (`return expand(pattern)`).
# EXPOSURE: the DoS needs attacker-controlled brace patterns; these copies only
# ever expand our own lint globs, in a dev-time tool that is not on any
# runtime path. Revisit when eslint v10 lands (blocklisted separately) and
# drops the minimatch@3 chain.
